 among cards, helping to identify Flushes, Straight Flushes, and Royal Flushes. For example, a count closer to 5 indicates a potential Flush, while lower counts mean suits are more diverse and less likely to be part of flush-related hands.\n\ndf['suit_match_count'] = df[['V3', 'V5', 'V9']].apply(lambda row: sum(row.duplicated(keep=False)), axis=1)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Feature name: high_rank_ratio\n# Feature description: Proportion of high-value ranks (10, Jack, Queen, King, Ace) among the five cards.\n# Usefulness: High-value ranks are essential in determining hands like Royal Flush, Straight, and Full House. For example, hands containing a higher proportion of these ranks have an increased likelihood of forming poker hands like Royal Flushes or Straights that include high-value cards.\n\ndf['high_rank_ratio'] = df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']].apply(lambda row: sum(row >= 10) / len(row), axis=1)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column `V10` (Rank of Card #5) is dropped\n# Reason: The individual rank of a single card contributes less compared to features that summarize patterns across the hand (e.g., rank_gap_std, high_rank_ratio). Single-card rank values may overfit for specific scenarios and reduce generalization for downstream models.\ndf.drop(columns=['V10'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column `rank_frequency_encoding` is dropped\n# Reason: While rank_frequency_encoding provides information about rank occurrences, its categorical nature doesn't capture relative rank distributions or sequential patterns that are crucial for poker hand identification. Features like rank_gap_std or high_rank_ratio encapsulate this information more effectively.\ndf.drop(columns=['rank_frequency_encoding'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column `unique_suit_count` is dropped\n# Reason: unique_suit_count provides only the number of unique suits but doesn't capture the exact counts or distribution, which are better addressed by features like suit_match_count. Removing it simplifies the feature space without significant loss of predictive value.\ndf.drop(columns=['unique_suit_count'], inplace=True)\n```end"]
