2026-05-13 23:02:50,587 - INFO - Selected state: ("Here are the next steps to generate additional features and improve the downstream model's performance:\n\n### Possible next steps:\n\npython\n# Step 1: Create a 'Same Suit Count' feature\n# Feature name: same_suit_count\n# Feature description: Count the number of cards in each hand with the same suit.\ndf['same_suit_count'] = df[['V1', 'V3', 'V5', 'V7', 'V9']].apply(lambda x: x.value_counts().max(), axis=1)\n# Usefulness: Poker hands like Flush, Straight Flush, and Royal Flush depend on cards having the same suit. \n# This feature highlights whether the hand has potential to contain these hands.\nend\n\npython\n# Step 2: Create a 'Sequential Rank Check' feature\n# Feature name: is_sequential_rank\n# Feature description: Boolean feature that checks whether the ranks of the cards are sequential.\ndf['is_sequential_rank'] = df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']].apply(lambda x: sorted(x), axis=1).apply(lambda x: all(x[i+1] - x[i] == 1 for i in range(len(x) - 1)))\n# Usefulness: Detecting sequential rank is critical for hands such as Straight, Straight Flush, and Royal Flush, \n# which require cards to be sequentially ranked without gaps.\nend\n\npython\n# Step 3: Create a 'Rank Frequency Encoding' feature\n# Feature name: rank_frequency_encoding\n# Feature description: Create a feature encoding the frequency of each rank in the hand.\ndf['rank_frequency_encoding'] = df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']].apply(lambda x: ','.join(map(str, sorted(x.value_counts()))), axis=1)\n# Usefulness: This feature can help identify patterns such as pairs (1,1), three of a kind (1,1,3), two pairs (1,2,2), four of a kind (4,1), \n# and full house (2,3). It gives a compact representation of rank frequency for better model interpretability.\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropped columns:\n\nIf any columns are redundant after feature engineering, we explain their removal below.\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'XX' is dropped\n# No columns are dropped at this stage as the original predictive attributes are necessary for feature engineering.\n# Dropping may occur after testing all new features and optimizing model performance metrics.\nend\n\n### Summary of feature usefulness:\n\n1. same_suit_count: Highlights the potential for hands requiring uniform suit cards.\n2. is_sequential_rank: Essential for predicting Straight-based poker hands.\n3. rank_frequency_encoding: Encapsulates frequency information of ranks useful for predicting patterns like Full House, Two Pairs, etc.\n\nThese additional features provide more structured insights into patterns within the poker hands, aiding the model in accurately distinguishing between complex poker hand categories.", "Here are three possible next steps for feature engineering to improve the downstream model's performance. These steps aim to capture deeper relationships between the cards and poker hand patterns based on domain knowledge.\n\n### Step 1: Generate a feature for the number of unique ranks\nThis feature captures how diverse the card ranks are in the hand, which can help identify patterns like pairs, three of a kind, full house, etc.\n\npython\n# unique_rank_count\n# Feature description: The number of unique ranks among the five cards.\n# Usefulness: A lower count of unique ranks can indicate pairs, three/four of a kind, or a full house. For example, if there are only two unique ranks, it could signal a full house or four of a kind.\ndf['unique_rank_count'] = df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']].nunique(axis=1)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Generate a feature for the maximum consecutive rank difference\nThis feature helps capture whether the ranks in the hand form a sequence (useful for straights or straight flushes).\n\npython\n# max_consecutive_rank_diff\n# Feature description: The largest difference between consecutive ranks when the ranks are sorted.\n# Usefulness: For straights or straight flushes, this value should be 1 consistently across all consecutive pairs of ranks. Any gap >1 indicates the hand is not sequential.\ndf['max_consecutive_rank_diff'] = df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']].apply(\n    lambda row: max(sorted(row) - sorted(row[:-1])), axis=1\n)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Create a feature for suit diversity\nThis feature calculates the number of unique suits in the hand, which is important for detecting flushes or mixed-suit hands.\n\npython\n# unique_suit_count\n# Feature description: The number of unique suits among the five cards.\n# Usefulness: A hand consisting of only one suit (unique suit count = 1) is essential for flushes, straight flushes, or royal flushes, while higher diversity can rule out those hands.\ndf['unique_suit_count'] = df[['V1', 'V3', 'V5', 'V7', 'V9']].nunique(axis=1)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Drop Irrelevant/Redundant Features\nThe same_suit_count feature may become redundant after adding unique_suit_count, as they provide overlapping information about suit homogeneity.\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column same_suit_count is dropped\n# The same_suit_count feature is replaced by unique_suit_count, which provides similar information but can distinguish between hands with variations like 2 suits versus 3 suits (e.g., for mixed flushes).\ndf.drop(columns=['same_suit_count'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Summary of New Features\n1. unique_rank_count: Helps classify pairs, three-of-a-kinds, full houses, etc.\n2. max_consecutive_rank_diff: Identifies whether ranks are sequential (essential for straights).\n3. unique_suit_count: Captures suit diversity, improving flush detection.", "Here's the detailed response designed to improve downstream task predictions by generating new features and dropping less useful ones:\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Adding Sequential Rank Gap Feature\nThis feature ensures a deeper understanding of gaps between consecutive ranks for detecting hands like straights or straight flushes.\n\npython\n# sequential_rank_gaps\n# Feature description: The absolute difference between consecutive card ranks to identify gaps.\n# Usefulness: Identifying gaps between ranks is crucial for predicting straights or straight flushes. For example, sequential gaps of {1, 1, 1, 1} may strongly indicate a straight, while larger gaps (e.g., {2, 3, 1}) break sequential order. This is directly relevant to target categories such as straight, straight flush, and royal flush.\ndf['sequential_rank_gaps'] = df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']].diff(axis=1).abs().sum(axis=1)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Adding Same Suit Ratio Feature\nThis feature helps assess the likelihood of flush-related poker hands by analyzing suit uniformity.\n\npython\n# same_suit_ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of cards with the same suit to the total number of cards.\n# Usefulness: This feature highlights the concentration of cards with identical suits. A higher ratio (e.g., 1 or close to 1) strongly correlates with flush hands, including royal flush and straight flush. It improves detection of higher-ranked hands effectively.\ndf['same_suit_ratio'] = df.groupby(df[['V3', 'V5', 'V9']].idxmax(axis=1)).transform(lambda x: x / x.sum(), axis=  ))\n END", "Here are the next three steps for feature engineering and dropping columns based on the dataset attributes and task requirements.\n\n### Step 1: Generate a Feature for Rank Suit Interactions\npython\n# rank_suit_interaction\n# Feature description: Create an interaction feature combining rank and suit for each card to capture specific card information in a denser representation.\n# Usefulness: This feature will allow the model to better understand combinations of rank and suit that make specific hands like Royal Flush or Straight Flush easier to identify. Combining rank and suit interactions for each card provides more granular information to help predict hands.\n\nfor i in range(1, 6):  # Loop through cards 1 to 5\n    df[f'rank_suit_interaction_card{i}'] = df[f'V{i * 2}'].astype(str) + '_' + df[f'V{i * 2 - 1}'].astype(str)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Sequential Rank Gaps Transformation\npython\n# normalized_sequential_rank_gaps\n# Feature description: Normalize the existing sequential rank gap values using zscore to capture deviation from the mean or median gaps.\n# Usefulness: The size and pattern of gaps between consecutive card ranks (captured by sequential_rank_gaps) strongly influence the identification of hands like Straights or Straight Flush. Normalization using zscores can help highlight significant outliers, which are often indicative of specific poker hands like straights. \n\ndf['normalized_sequential_rank_gaps'] = (df['sequential_rank_gaps'] - df['sequential_rank_gaps'].mean()) / df['sequential_rank_gaps'].std()\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Count High Cards (Ace, King, Queen, Jack, Ten)\npython\n# high_card_count\n# Feature description: Count the number of high cards (Ace, King, Queen, Jack, Ten) in a hand.\n# Usefulness: High cards are central to identifying hands like Royal Flush or Straight Flush. By counting high cards explicitly, the model gains an intuitive representation of hands likely to contain stronger poker rankings.\n\nhigh_card_ranks = {1, 10, 11, 12, 13}  # Ace, Ten, Jack, Queen, King\ndf['high_card_count'] = df.apply(lambda row: sum(rank in high_card_ranks for rank in [row['V2'], row['V4'], row['V6'], row['V8'], row['V10']]), axis=1)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropped Column: sequential_rank_gaps (if normalized is sufficient)\npython\n# Explanation why the column sequential_rank_gaps is dropped\n# Sequential rank gaps have been normalized into the feature normalized_sequential_rank_gaps, which captures the essential information in a more structured way. Dropping the original column avoids redundancy and reduces dimensionality.\n\ndf.drop(columns=['sequential_rank_gaps'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Conclusion:\nEach added feature improves the model's ability to extract meaningful patterns for predicting poker hands:\n1. Rank-suit interaction allows richer representation of card specifics.\n2. Normalized sequential rank gaps emphasize gaps relevant for straights and similar patterns.\n3. High card count focuses explicitly on identifying strong hands such as Royal Flush."), with improvements -
